course: dictionary
lesson: 6374
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, look at the context: the words and sentences around it. Muffin can mean a cake, a flat bread roll, or a teasing name for someone.
explain: «Picture muffins coming out of the oven with green blueberries» tells you that muffins are baked in an oven, and blueberries are fruit, so here a muffin is a cake. A bread roll would not have blueberries in it.
explain: Ask yourself: which meaning fits this sentence? Then swap your meaning in and check that it still makes sense.
fact: A word with more than one meaning is a word with several meanings. You find the right one from its context.
fact: Muffin meaning 1: a small sweet cake baked in a cup-shaped tin, often with fruit or chocolate chips.
fact: Muffin meaning 2: in the UK, an English muffin is a flat, round bread roll, eaten toasted with butter.
fact: Muffin meaning 3: informally, muffin can be a friendly or teasing name for someone.
exercise: Mum toasted a muffin and spread butter on it for breakfast. What is a muffin here? | A tin for baking | *A flat, round bread roll | A small sweet cake with chocolate chips | A teasing name for someone
exercise: «Sam, you muffin, you have left your bag on the bus again!» Said with a grin, what does muffin mean? | *A friendly, teasing name | A sweet cake | A bread roll | A baking tin
exercise: In «Picture muffins coming out of the oven with green blueberries», which word tells you the muffins were baked? | *oven | green | blueberries | Picture
test: In «12 muffins shared among 4 children», what is a muffin? | A teasing name | A cup of tea | *A small sweet cake | A flat bread roll for toast
test: «The English muffin was split, toasted and spread with jam.» What is an English muffin here? | A small cake with fruit in it | A friendly name for a friend | A type of biscuit | *A flat, round bread roll
exam: Jayden says muffin only ever means a cake. Is he right? | Yes, muffin only ever means a cake | *No, it can also mean a bread roll or a teasing name | No, it means a baking tin | Yes, but only in the UK
